WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:03.894
Here's the question.

2
00:00:03.894 --> 00:00:07.475
You have one setting, the greeting our little web app shows.

3
00:00:07.475 --> 00:00:09.444
It's set in four different files.

4
00:00:09.444 --> 00:00:11.772
Which one does the server actually get?

5
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.310
You might guess the last one written, or the first one read.

6
00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:17.517
Let's go back to the street to find out.

7
00:00:18.233 --> 00:00:22.656
Our house sitter now looks after a whole street, and the owners start leaving notes.

8
00:00:22.867 --> 00:00:26.099
The printed checklist says: heating at twenty degrees.

9
00:00:26.099 --> 00:00:28.853
That's the author's suggestion, for any house.

10
00:00:29.067 --> 00:00:32.529
Then a note for this side of the street: nineteen degrees.

11
00:00:32.733 --> 00:00:36.153
And a sticky note on one front door: twenty-two, please.

12
00:00:36.367 --> 00:00:38.174
Then the owners phone tonight.

13
00:00:38.174 --> 00:00:40.583
Eighteen degrees everywhere, we're away.

14
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.783
Four notes, and they disagree.

15
00:00:42.783 --> 00:00:45.559
A real sitter would ring somebody and ask.

16
00:00:45.559 --> 00:00:46.815
Ansible never asks.

17
00:00:46.815 --> 00:00:49.723
It picks a winner by a fixed list, silently.

18
00:00:50.633 --> 00:00:52.602
Here's that list, as a ladder.

19
00:00:52.602 --> 00:00:55.489
Weakest at the bottom, strongest at the top.

20
00:00:55.700 --> 00:01:00.103
The printed checklist is the role's defaults, the author's suggestions.

21
00:01:00.103 --> 00:01:01.964
So it sits at the very bottom.

22
00:01:02.167 --> 00:01:06.568
The note for one side of the street is a group's file in group_vars.

23
00:01:06.568 --> 00:01:09.028
One file for all hosts, one per group.

24
00:01:09.233 --> 00:01:13.241
The sticky note on one door is host_vars, a file for one host.

25
00:01:13.241 --> 00:01:17.119
One host is more specific than its group, so it sits higher.

26
00:01:17.333 --> 00:01:21.692
Values written in the playbook itself beat everything in the inventory.

27
00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:27.163
And the phone call tonight is minus e, extra vars typed on the command line.

28
00:01:27.163 --> 00:01:28.271
They always win.

29
00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:30.238
I've left one gap on purpose.

30
00:01:30.238 --> 00:01:32.695
Hold that thought, we'll come back to it.

31
00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:35.071
Now the real thing.

32
00:01:35.071 --> 00:01:40.988
The lab's two web servers, web1 and web2, and one variable, hello_devops_greeting.

33
00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:44.429
The role's default says: Hello, DevOps!

34
00:01:44.633 --> 00:01:47.648
The web group's file says: Hello from Ansible.

35
00:01:47.867 --> 00:01:51.449
And web2's own file says: Hello from web2, the canary.

36
00:01:51.449 --> 00:01:58.547
A canary is the one server that gets a change first, so a problem shows up on one machine, not all of them.

37
00:01:58.767 --> 00:02:01.874
The debug module asks each host what it sees.

38
00:02:01.874 --> 00:02:06.087
Before we run it: what does web1 get, and what does web2 get?

39
00:02:06.087 --> 00:02:07.192
Pause and guess.

40
00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:14.911
web1 isn't in host_vars, so its strongest note is the group's: Hello from Ansible.

41
00:02:15.133 --> 00:02:19.987
web2 has its own note, one rung higher, so it gets the canary text.

42
00:02:19.987 --> 00:02:21.508
And the role default?

43
00:02:21.508 --> 00:02:22.595
Beaten by both.

44
00:02:22.595 --> 00:02:24.696
It's a suggestion, after all.

45
00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:26.422
Now add a phone call.

46
00:02:26.422 --> 00:02:30.119
Run the same command with minus e, Hi from minus e.

47
00:02:30.333 --> 00:02:32.451
Both hosts print Hi from minus e.

48
00:02:32.451 --> 00:02:35.403
The canary's sticky note is still on the door.

49
00:02:35.403 --> 00:02:37.713
Tonight, the phone call simply wins.

50
00:02:38.833 --> 00:02:47.115
This ladder has a name: variable precedence, the rule that decides which value wins when one variable is set in several places.

51
00:02:47.115 --> 00:02:49.202
The docs list twenty-two levels.

52
00:02:49.202 --> 00:02:51.419
In practice, you need these seven.

53
00:02:52.033 --> 00:02:56.766
So far, a tidy story: the more specific the note, the stronger it is.

54
00:02:56.766 --> 00:02:58.617
Now back to the gap I left.

55
00:02:59.433 --> 00:03:02.225
Role vars, in the role's own vars folder.

56
00:03:02.225 --> 00:03:05.697
They sit above group_vars, and above host_vars too.

57
00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:08.568
Imagine someone puts the greeting in there.

58
00:03:08.568 --> 00:03:10.367
What does the canary get now?

59
00:03:10.367 --> 00:03:11.360
Pause and guess.

60
00:03:13.567 --> 00:03:15.548
Both hosts get the role's text.

61
00:03:15.548 --> 00:03:20.023
The canary's own file is ignored, and Ansible gives no warning at all.

62
00:03:20.233 --> 00:03:20.697
Well...

63
00:03:20.697 --> 00:03:23.145
the sticky note is still on the door.

64
00:03:23.145 --> 00:03:24.733
Nobody reads it anymore.

65
00:03:24.733 --> 00:03:28.836
A forgotten override like that can hide your change for weeks.

66
00:03:29.967 --> 00:03:31.534
So here's the guardrail.

67
00:03:31.534 --> 00:03:35.909
The defaults folder is for every setting a user may want to change.

68
00:03:35.909 --> 00:03:41.656
The vars folder is only for the role's own fixed details, things nobody should override.

69
00:03:41.867 --> 00:03:44.844
And picture the same name sitting in four files.

70
00:03:44.844 --> 00:03:50.612
Can anyone predict the result without opening all four and climbing the ladder in their head?

71
00:03:50.612 --> 00:03:51.295
Nobody can.

72
00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:55.848
So the rule: set each variable in one place, plus its role default.

73
00:03:55.848 --> 00:03:58.703
And when in doubt, ask the hosts with debug.

74
00:03:59.833 --> 00:04:01.611
Let's climb it once more.

75
00:04:01.611 --> 00:04:03.744
Defaults are only suggestions.

76
00:04:03.744 --> 00:04:05.379
A host beats its group.

77
00:04:05.379 --> 00:04:07.725
The playbook beats the inventory.

78
00:04:07.725 --> 00:04:10.142
Role vars beat them all, silently.

79
00:04:10.142 --> 00:04:13.057
And the phone call, minus e, always wins.

80
00:04:13.267 --> 00:04:17.539
The moral of the story: Ansible never asks which note you meant.

81
00:04:17.539 --> 00:04:19.808
So leave it only one note to read.

82
00:04:21.033 --> 00:04:22.127
A quick check.

83
00:04:22.127 --> 00:04:24.313
The role default says Hello.

84
00:04:24.313 --> 00:04:26.031
The web group says Hi.

85
00:04:26.031 --> 00:04:27.983
web2's own file says Hey.

86
00:04:27.983 --> 00:04:29.467
What does web2 get?

87
00:04:29.467 --> 00:04:30.716
Pause and think.

88
00:04:32.933 --> 00:04:33.220
Hey.

89
00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:38.451
One host is more specific than its group, and both beat the role default.
